Banana Docks, Teddy Roosevelt,
slippery banana peels,

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:55.627
silly newspaper articles
from the late 1800s.

00:05:55.627 --> 00:05:58.615
This isn't the story here.
We're just getting started.

00:05:58.615 --> 00:06:01.282
This is banana's innocent phase.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:05.313
It's about to heat up.

00:06:05.313 --> 00:06:09.230
Let's see how bananas
turned from this to this.

00:06:10.172 --> 00:06:12.839
(upbeat music)

00:06:14.327 --> 00:06:16.413
The banana business
people in the US realized

00:06:16.413 --> 00:06:19.774
it was time to ramp up to
meet all of this new demand.

00:06:19.774 --> 00:06:22.579
But instead of just importing
more bananas from say

00:06:22.579 --> 00:06:24.804
Jamaica or other countries
in the Caribbean,

00:06:24.804 --> 00:06:26.663
the banana companies realized

00:06:26.663 --> 00:06:30.261
that they needed to
control the supply chain.

00:06:30.261 --> 00:06:32.361
Let me explain what I mean by second,

00:06:32.361 --> 00:06:33.584
by looking at this banana.

00:06:33.584 --> 00:06:36.837
Bananas are fragile. They're soft.

00:06:36.837 --> 00:06:38.265
They spoil in like a week,

00:06:38.265 --> 00:06:40.174
but this one's like
moments away from spoiling.

00:06:40.174 --> 00:06:41.299
Look at those spots.

00:06:41.299 --> 00:06:43.988
To get this thing from
somewhere close to the equator

00:06:43.988 --> 00:06:47.339
all the way to New York City
and then into someone's market

00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:51.406
and then into somebody's home
all without it being crushed

00:06:51.406 --> 00:06:54.273
and without it spoiling is a fragile,

00:06:54.273 --> 00:06:56.422
quick, expensive process.

00:06:56.422 --> 00:07:00.240
And the only way to do it
profitably is to do a lot of it.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:02.099
A lot of bananas.

00:07:02.099 --> 00:07:04.689
(upbeat music)

00:07:04.689 --> 00:07:06.226
And the only way to do a lot of bananas

00:07:06.226 --> 00:07:09.536
is to control the supply chain.

00:07:09.536 --> 00:07:11.467
So it's like 1900 at this point,

00:07:11.467 --> 00:07:13.026
there's a banana gold rush happening.

00:07:13.026 --> 00:07:15.053
And there's a bunch of
companies trying to figure out

00:07:15.053 --> 00:07:16.275
how to control the supply chain.

00:07:16.275 --> 00:07:18.483
And then they decided to sort
of merge together and become

00:07:18.483 --> 00:07:22.722
this one super company called
the United Fruit Company.

00:07:22.722 --> 00:07:24.939
And their plan for combining was to

00:07:24.939 --> 00:07:28.504
control everything,
control the supply chain.

00:07:28.504 --> 00:07:31.731
They turned their attention
completely to Central America

00:07:31.731 --> 00:07:34.028
where they know bananas
could grow abundantly,

00:07:34.028 --> 00:07:37.068
and where, oh, look at this.

00:07:37.068 --> 00:07:39.273
The US is thinking about
making a little canal that cuts

00:07:39.273 --> 00:07:41.568
through this little narrow strip of land.

00:07:41.568 --> 00:07:43.458
So the United Fruit Company is like,

00:07:43.458 --> 00:07:45.604
that's kind of a nice perk for the region.

00:07:45.604 --> 00:07:46.614
If anything goes wrong,

00:07:46.614 --> 00:07:49.209
the US could have our backs.

00:07:49.209 --> 00:07:50.478
Foreshadowing.

00:07:50.478 --> 00:07:53.200
Am I foreshadowing? I'm foreshadowing.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:54.451
So yeah, United Fruit Company is like,

00:07:54.451 --> 00:07:55.987
this region, central America ,

00:07:55.987 --> 00:08:00.090
looks like a great neighborhood
to control the supply chain.

00:08:00.090 --> 00:08:01.983
They wanted to control
the people who worked

00:08:01.983 --> 00:08:04.757
on the farm by owning
their basic survival needs.

00:08:04.757 --> 00:08:07.275
They wanted to control
the houses they lived in

00:08:07.275 --> 00:08:09.595
and the stores they shopped
in and what they could buy.

00:08:09.595 --> 00:08:11.901
They wanted to control the
transportation by building

00:08:11.901 --> 00:08:14.826
railways so that they could quickly ship

00:08:14.826 --> 00:08:16.237
their product onto ports.

00:08:16.237 --> 00:08:19.828
They wanted to control the
boats and the waterways so that

00:08:19.828 --> 00:08:22.604
they could get all of these
bananas from Central America

00:08:22.604 --> 00:08:25.244
to the US before they spoiled.

00:08:25.244 --> 00:08:27.627
They started with Guatemala,

00:08:27.627 --> 00:08:28.553
pouring tons of investment into

00:08:28.553 --> 00:08:31.827
controlling every inch
of their supply chain.

00:08:31.827 --> 00:08:35.512
Soon, they were the largest
company in Guatemala.

00:08:35.512 --> 00:08:39.162
They owned a fifth of the
farmable land in the country.

00:08:39.162 --> 00:08:41.921
They owned all of the railways and

00:08:41.921 --> 00:08:44.752
all of the radio stations
and radio infrastructure.

00:08:44.752 --> 00:08:46.013
And by 1901,

00:08:46.013 --> 00:08:48.928
the government of Guatemala
actually hired the

00:08:48.928 --> 00:08:53.333
United Fruit Company to manage
the country's postal service.

00:08:53.333 --> 00:08:54.166
What?

00:08:54.166 --> 00:08:57.807
United Fruit was starting to
look a lot like a government.

00:08:57.807 --> 00:09:02.512
And the result was a lot of
happy banana eating Americans.

00:09:02.512 --> 00:09:03.679
So they kept going.

00:09:03.679 --> 00:09:07.190
They kept expanding their
operations across Central America.

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:09.603
So now with all of this
beautiful infrastructure

00:09:09.603 --> 00:09:11.237
and trains and land,

00:09:11.237 --> 00:09:14.149
the next strategy for making
bananas even more profitable

00:09:14.149 --> 00:09:19.133
was, you guessed it, paying
the workers next to nothing.

00:09:19.133 --> 00:09:22.166
And paying them not with real money.

00:09:22.166 --> 00:09:22.999
(upbeat music)

00:09:22.999 --> 00:09:24.319
A lot of the times United Fruit

00:09:24.319 --> 00:09:26.618
paid their workers in vouchers.

00:09:26.618 --> 00:09:28.802
These vouchers could only be used

00:09:28.802 --> 00:09:31.936
in designated United Fruit commissaries.

00:09:31.936 --> 00:09:34.782
So they're not actually
making real money here.

00:09:34.782 --> 00:09:37.952
So wait, now the United fruit
Company has its own currency?

00:09:37.952 --> 00:09:40.419
Oh, and they also had like a private Navy,

00:09:40.419 --> 00:09:43.662
93 boats called the Great White Fleet.

00:09:43.662 --> 00:09:46.189
Then eventually they
started using these boats

00:09:46.189 --> 00:09:48.495
to transport people on cruises.

00:09:48.495 --> 00:09:51.465
These boats were even
used during World War II.

00:09:51.465 --> 00:09:55.043
United Fruit started to look
like a literal government.

00:09:55.043 --> 00:09:57.623
They had their hands in
everything so much so

00:09:57.623 --> 00:10:00.469
that they earned their
self the nickname El Pulpo,

00:10:00.469 --> 00:10:02.566
which means the octopus in Spanish,

00:10:02.566 --> 00:10:04.980
meaning they had their
tentacles in everything

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:07.714
all over Central America, the
land, the crops, the people,

00:10:07.714 --> 00:10:11.507
the infrastructure, and soon
enough government agencies.

00:10:11.507 --> 00:10:14.314
Soon these countries
became so dominated by

00:10:14.314 --> 00:10:18.222
and reliant on the United Fruit Company

00:10:18.222 --> 00:10:20.252
that they were no longer run

00:10:20.252 --> 00:10:22.245
by the people or the governments.

00:10:22.245 --> 00:10:24.860
They were run by American banana companies

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:27.720
who had all the power and
leverage in the world.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:30.560
This led to the nickname Banana Republic,

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:33.801
which is a politically unstable
country whose society is

00:10:33.801 --> 00:10:37.114
exploited for profit
for one single product,

00:10:37.114 --> 00:10:39.311
in this case bananas.

00:10:39.311 --> 00:10:41.972
And probably not the best
sort of thing to name

00:10:41.972 --> 00:10:44.160
your clothing store after,

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:48.106
but just a thought, but
like who made that decision?

00:10:48.106 --> 00:10:50.279
United Fruit and other banana companies

00:10:50.279 --> 00:10:53.010
continued to grow and
control Central America.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.810
Eventually people got tired of this.

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:59.380
In 1911, Honduras was like,
all right, we're done,

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:00.213
banana companies.

00:11:00.213 --> 00:11:02.606
We're going to take back our land.

00:11:02.606 --> 00:11:05.180
We can take it from here.
These are our plantations.

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:07.855
You've gone too far. Banana
companies didn't like this.

00:11:07.855 --> 00:11:10.819
So they organized a private
army to help overthrow the

00:11:10.819 --> 00:11:13.302
government so that they
could put in a president

00:11:13.302 --> 00:11:15.127
that they liked who would allow them

00:11:15.127 --> 00:11:16.193
to keep doing exactly what they

00:11:16.193 --> 00:11:19.279
were doing and also give them a tax break.

00:11:19.279 --> 00:11:20.112
So yeah,

00:11:20.112 --> 00:11:23.120
banana companies are now
overthrowing governments.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:23.953
Jeez.

00:11:23.953 --> 00:11:24.786
This kept happening.

00:11:24.786 --> 00:11:27.366
Anytime there was political
dissent or the governments

00:11:27.366 --> 00:11:30.062
of these countries started
to step up and say no,

00:11:30.062 --> 00:11:32.319
the banana companies would intervene,

00:11:32.319 --> 00:11:34.207
and guess who had their back?

00:11:34.207 --> 00:11:36.953
The US military, and yes,

00:11:36.953 --> 00:11:39.525
we're back to Teddy Roosevelt.

00:11:39.525 --> 00:11:41.157
Teddy Roosevelt.

00:11:41.157 --> 00:11:42.308
What a guy.

00:11:42.308 --> 00:11:43.764
Here's Teddy stomping around the

00:11:43.764 --> 00:11:45.716
Caribbean with his big stick,

00:11:45.716 --> 00:11:49.637
making sure that American
military and business interests

00:11:49.637 --> 00:11:52.294
are protected, in Honduras alone,

00:11:52.294 --> 00:11:56.881
the US invaded seven
times in the early 1900s,

00:11:56.881 --> 00:11:59.845
all in the name of protecting
these banana companies

00:11:59.845 --> 00:12:01.259
and other US interests

00:12:01.259 --> 00:12:04.583
from having to face these pesky locals

00:12:04.583 --> 00:12:08.796
who were so audacious as to
want to run their own country.

00:12:08.796 --> 00:12:09.695
How dare they?

00:12:09.695 --> 00:12:11.844
If you remember that at this time,

00:12:11.844 --> 00:12:14.084
the US was starting to get
really comfortable with empire

00:12:14.084 --> 00:12:16.654
behavior and central America

00:12:16.654 --> 00:12:18.692
was at the top of the priority list.

00:12:18.692 --> 00:12:20.070
The banana companies knew this,

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:21.861
which allowed them to feel emboldened,

00:12:21.861 --> 00:12:25.017
to just sort of do whatever
they wanted in the region.

00:12:25.017 --> 00:12:26.877
But this is sort of child's play.

00:12:26.877 --> 00:12:30.886
It started to get a
lot worse in the 1920s.

00:12:30.886 --> 00:12:32.252
(upbeat music)

00:12:32.252 --> 00:12:35.438
There was a situation in
Colombia where a bunch of workers

00:12:35.438 --> 00:12:38.661
for the United Fruit Company
decided to stop working

00:12:38.661 --> 00:12:40.920
and protest their working conditions.

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.609
They were asking for a few
things like, I don't know,

00:12:43.609 --> 00:12:46.622
working six days a week instead of seven,

00:12:46.622 --> 00:12:49.004
or getting paid real money.

00:12:49.004 --> 00:12:51.912
The United Fruit Company
refuses to negotiate with them.

00:12:51.912 --> 00:12:55.151
And instead goes to the
US and says, hey, USA,

00:12:55.151 --> 00:12:58.089
we've got these really
annoying workers who are

00:12:58.089 --> 00:13:01.139
trying to unionize and
trying to get paid and stuff.

00:13:01.139 --> 00:13:05.388
And it actually smells
a lot like communism.

00:13:05.388 --> 00:13:07.464
And the US is like, wait, what, communism?

00:13:07.464 --> 00:13:08.297
Tell me more.

00:13:08.297 --> 00:13:10.954
So then the US threatens to invade

00:13:10.954 --> 00:13:13.392
with their Marines and squash this strike

00:13:13.392 --> 00:13:16.440
if the Colombian military
doesn't do something first.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.481
Reminder that we're not talking about

00:13:18.481 --> 00:13:21.264
some big political
revolution or rebellion.

00:13:21.264 --> 00:13:25.342
We're talking about a few
workers in a banana plantation

00:13:25.342 --> 00:13:28.306
protesting for more humane conditions.

00:13:28.306 --> 00:13:31.309
And yet this was a priority
for the US government

00:13:31.309 --> 00:13:35.387
to put pressure on Colombia
to fix the situation.

00:13:35.387 --> 00:13:38.316
Of course, Columbia didn't
want to make the US angry.

00:13:38.316 --> 00:13:41.024
So they responded and
sent in their own troops

00:13:41.024 --> 00:13:43.748
to go put down this workers' strike.

00:13:43.748 --> 00:13:45.408
And they were ordered to quote,

00:13:45.408 --> 00:13:47.075
spare no ammunition.

00:13:49.309 --> 00:13:51.012
So on December 5th, 1928,

00:13:51.012 --> 00:13:53.509
these protesters are in the town square

00:13:53.509 --> 00:13:54.394
in this town in Columbia.

00:13:54.394 --> 00:13:58.918
And the Colombian army
shows up and massacres them.

00:13:58.918 --> 00:14:03.251
Men, women, and children were
killed by their own military

00:14:03.251 --> 00:14:05.748
all because an American banana company

00:14:05.748 --> 00:14:07.977
didn't want to pay them a decent wage.

00:14:07.977 --> 00:14:09.984
I mean, this is madness.

00:14:09.984 --> 00:14:14.278
It is madness to think that
these large geopolitical forces

00:14:14.278 --> 00:14:17.075
were coming to bear over
a banana plantation.

00:14:17.075 --> 00:14:20.591
This event is called the Banana Massacre.

00:14:20.591 --> 00:14:22.526
This didn't stop in 1928,

00:14:22.526 --> 00:14:25.347
fast forward to Guatemala in the 1950s.

00:14:25.347 --> 00:14:26.180
At this point,

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:28.437
the United Fruit Company, El Pulpo,

00:14:28.437 --> 00:14:30.540
is making major profits.

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:35.540
They own almost 50% of the
land in Guatemala tax-free.

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.126
But this president, Jacobo Arbenz,

00:14:38.126 --> 00:14:41.867
who was democratically elected
is trying to change things.

00:14:41.867 --> 00:14:44.413
He wants to take land
that United Fruit owns

00:14:44.413 --> 00:14:47.677
but isn't using and redistribute
it to poor Guatemalans.

00:14:47.677 --> 00:14:50.052
He's sort of doing like
a Robin Hood thing,

00:14:50.052 --> 00:14:52.403
trying to like lift poor
Guatemalans out of poverty.

00:14:52.403 --> 00:14:54.590
But of course, United
Fruit didn't like this,

00:14:54.590 --> 00:14:56.911
but instead of engaging directly

00:14:56.911 --> 00:14:57.997
with the Guatemalan government,

00:14:57.997 --> 00:15:01.239
United Fruit goes to the white house

00:15:01.239 --> 00:15:04.404
and says those magic
words again, communism.

00:15:04.404 --> 00:15:07.728
United Fruit then hired this PR magician

00:15:07.728 --> 00:15:09.707
who happened to be Sigmund Freud's nephew.

00:15:09.707 --> 00:15:12.528
And he worked with news
agencies to create a bunch

00:15:12.528 --> 00:15:15.811
of fake stories that linked Arbenz,

00:15:15.811 --> 00:15:17.348
the president of Guatemala to communism,

00:15:17.348 --> 00:15:19.330
completely fake news.

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:21.322
And not just like using that word lightly,

00:15:21.322 --> 00:15:24.764
like he created a fake
Guatemalan newspaper,

00:15:24.764 --> 00:15:26.049
created all these fake reports

00:15:26.049 --> 00:15:29.925
and then distributed those
fake newspapers to Congress.

00:15:29.925 --> 00:15:31.649
He planted the seeds in their minds

00:15:31.649 --> 00:15:33.617
that United Fruit Company
were the good guys

00:15:33.617 --> 00:15:34.633
and that Arbenz,

00:15:34.633 --> 00:15:36.456
the democratically elected leader,

00:15:36.456 --> 00:15:39.266
needed to go because of communism.

00:15:39.266 --> 00:15:40.311
It totally worked.

00:15:40.311 --> 00:15:41.749
President Eisenhower,

00:15:41.749 --> 00:15:45.028
the president of the United
States believed all of this.

00:15:45.028 --> 00:15:49.740
And he sent in the CIA to
get Arbenz out of power

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:52.203
to protect the banana people once again.

00:15:52.203 --> 00:15:56.320
It's a classic CIA coup, they
go find a bunch of rebels.

00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:58.873
They give them money and they train them.

00:15:58.873 --> 00:16:00.290
- [Radio Announcer] Rebels rose to oust

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:02.526
Guatemala's red infiltrated government.

00:16:02.526 --> 00:16:04.817
- They find a leader who
wants to be the next president

00:16:04.817 --> 00:16:07.193
that's friendly to the US
and eventually they start

00:16:07.193 --> 00:16:09.785
broadcasting anti-government propaganda,

00:16:09.785 --> 00:16:12.700
and they turn Guatemalans
against their government

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:14.811
with all of this fake news and propaganda.

00:16:14.811 --> 00:16:17.430
They send the Navy in to
block Guatemalan waters.

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:20.356
They send some bombs onto Guatemala city,

00:16:20.356 --> 00:16:22.455
and then they invade.

00:16:22.455 --> 00:16:25.205
(dramatic music)

00:16:28.486 --> 00:16:30.145
And then with these trained fighters,

00:16:30.145 --> 00:16:31.946
they go take over the government,

00:16:31.946 --> 00:16:33.816
the Guatemalan army surrenders,

00:16:33.816 --> 00:16:36.996
and the leader of the rebels becomes

00:16:36.996 --> 00:16:39.083
the new president friendly
to the United States.

00:16:39.083 --> 00:16:41.991
And now the banana companies
are happy and they have a guy

00:16:41.991 --> 00:16:43.688
in power that is their guy.

00:16:43.688 --> 00:16:46.125
It's like they have a playbook on how to

00:16:46.125 --> 00:16:47.816
mess with democracies around the world.

00:16:47.816 --> 00:16:50.301
And they just sort of
followed the playbook.

00:16:50.301 --> 00:16:52.221
They're like, oh, we've done this before.

00:16:52.221 --> 00:16:55.115
We're going to do it again
in Iran in a little bit.

00:16:55.115 --> 00:16:58.198
This is classic CIA coup, by the way,

00:16:59.068 --> 00:17:01.658
this coup was sort of a death blow

00:17:01.658 --> 00:17:03.195
to democracy in Guatemala.

00:17:03.195 --> 00:17:07.577
It divided and destroyed
the budding civil society

00:17:07.577 --> 00:17:11.356
that has not allowed
Guatemala to recover since,

00:17:11.356 --> 00:17:12.220
all because these banana companies

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:14.730
wanted to control the supply chain.

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:17.684
These things leave scars, major scars,

00:17:17.684 --> 00:17:22.014
major marks on a country, scars
that are still felt today.

00:17:22.014 --> 00:17:25.240
Let's switch gears for a
second because all of these

00:17:25.240 --> 00:17:27.564
banana coups and banana republics

00:17:27.564 --> 00:17:30.986
and massacres is like horrendous.

00:17:30.986 --> 00:17:33.243
But during all of this violence

00:17:33.243 --> 00:17:35.643
and military invasions and
human rights violations,

00:17:35.643 --> 00:17:38.126
all of this meant that
the United Fruit Company

00:17:38.126 --> 00:17:40.610
was getting really, really
good at their business.

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:44.147
They were sending too many
bananas to the United States.

00:17:44.147 --> 00:17:45.998
There was a ton of bananas supplied,

00:17:45.998 --> 00:17:48.070
but demand wasn't rising fast enough.

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:52.616
The average American didn't
totally know how to use bananas.

00:17:52.616 --> 00:17:53.622
So the United Fruit Company

00:17:53.622 --> 00:17:57.561
creates this amazing marketing campaign.

00:17:57.561 --> 00:17:59.550
(upbeat music)

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:01.976
So now they're paying
doctors to write amazing

00:18:01.976 --> 00:18:04.604
academic journal articles about bananas.

00:18:04.604 --> 00:18:08.564
They cut a deal with
Kellogg's to market the cereal

00:18:08.564 --> 00:18:10.375
as something that pairs with bananas,

00:18:10.375 --> 00:18:11.936
which is something I still do today.

00:18:11.936 --> 00:18:15.257
I cut up my bananas and
I put them in my cereal

00:18:15.257 --> 00:18:17.275
because of this campaign.

00:18:17.275 --> 00:18:19.640
They targeted moms with small children.

00:18:19.640 --> 00:18:21.159
They made recipe books.

00:18:21.159 --> 00:18:26.073
They even created a guide on
how to decorate with bananas.

00:18:26.073 --> 00:18:26.906
- [TV Broadcaster]
Bananas can to be served

00:18:26.906 --> 00:18:29.372
in many different and
many attractive ways.

00:18:29.372 --> 00:18:31.197
Blended with fruits, nuts, and gelatin,

00:18:31.197 --> 00:18:34.157
they provide high food
value with nourishment.

00:18:34.157 --> 00:18:38.069
- But their biggest
success was Miss Chiquita.

00:18:38.069 --> 00:18:40.827
♪ I'm Chiquita Banana
and I've come to say ♪

00:18:40.827 --> 00:18:43.484
♪ bananas have to ripen
in a certain way. ♪

00:18:43.484 --> 00:18:47.134
♪ And when they're flecked with
brown and have a golden hue ♪

00:18:47.134 --> 00:18:49.941
♪ Bananas taste the best
and are the best for you. ♪

00:18:49.941 --> 00:18:51.285
- The American public fell in love

00:18:51.285 --> 00:18:53.634
with this lady and bananas.

00:18:53.634 --> 00:18:55.880
They had no idea that these bananas

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:58.087
that were being produced and
shipped and bought with blood,

00:18:58.087 --> 00:18:59.645
they just liked how they taste.

00:18:59.645 --> 00:19:01.400
But isn't that the case
with a lot of things

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:02.731
that we all consume every day.

00:19:02.731 --> 00:19:06.784
But there's one thing that US
imperialism and millions of

00:19:06.784 --> 00:19:11.284
dollars of marketing cannot
control, which is disease.

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:15.520
This is the part of the
story that I tell you

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:17.202
that the bananas that were all the rage

00:19:17.202 --> 00:19:19.539
during the 1900s are not the bananas

00:19:19.539 --> 00:19:21.654
that you're eating today.

00:19:21.654 --> 00:19:23.096
(upbeat music)

00:19:23.096 --> 00:19:26.537
Part of United Fruit Company's
efforts to control everything

00:19:26.537 --> 00:19:29.612
was standardizing the
genetics of the banana,

00:19:29.612 --> 00:19:31.939
making them all exactly the same.

00:19:31.939 --> 00:19:33.729
Monocultures is what we call that.

00:19:33.729 --> 00:19:37.471
The problem with monocultures
is that they are heaven

00:19:37.471 --> 00:19:40.821
for a disease because
you don't have to solve

00:19:40.821 --> 00:19:43.255
the same puzzle to infect another banana.

00:19:43.255 --> 00:19:45.105
If you infect one, you infect them all.

00:19:45.105 --> 00:19:47.971
And that's exactly what happened.

00:19:47.971 --> 00:19:50.263
Panama disease was a fungus that came in

00:19:50.263 --> 00:19:53.046
and destroyed banana plantations.

00:19:53.046 --> 00:19:55.973
The banana supply in
the US shrank rapidly.

00:19:55.973 --> 00:20:00.110
And there was even a hit song
about the banana shortage.

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:03.693
♪ We have no bananas today ♪

00:20:07.601 --> 00:20:09.497
The banana that they had been using,

00:20:09.497 --> 00:20:11.265
the one that was getting
really popular in the US

00:20:11.265 --> 00:20:14.660
was called the Gros Michel,
or the Big Mike banana,

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:17.648
but it was now completely
getting ravaged by disease.

00:20:17.648 --> 00:20:19.877
So these banana companies
are freaking out.

00:20:19.877 --> 00:20:20.937
They're like, we are done,

00:20:20.937 --> 00:20:23.184
we've built this entire empire

00:20:23.184 --> 00:20:25.438
and this disease is going to wipe us out.

00:20:25.438 --> 00:20:28.558
So they started to scramble
to find another banana.

00:20:28.558 --> 00:20:29.391
It turns out there's like

00:20:29.391 --> 00:20:31.547
a thousand types of bananas out there.

00:20:31.547 --> 00:20:35.267
There's red ones and
blue ones and teal ones.

00:20:35.267 --> 00:20:36.605
I mean, a blue banana.

00:20:36.605 --> 00:20:38.399
I feel like I have to eat a blue banana

00:20:38.399 --> 00:20:40.348
at some point in my life,
but here's the problem.

00:20:40.348 --> 00:20:44.030
Us Americans, we buy our food
kind of with our eyes first.

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:45.912
We're just like, it needs to look good.

00:20:45.912 --> 00:20:48.338
And so these companies
realized they couldn't

00:20:48.338 --> 00:20:50.192
send a blue banana to the United States.

00:20:50.192 --> 00:20:52.873
They needed a banana that
looked similar, yellow,

00:20:52.873 --> 00:20:55.714
sweet and seedless so that
they could keep selling to

00:20:55.714 --> 00:20:59.090
Americans who hopefully
wouldn't notice the switch.

00:20:59.090 --> 00:21:02.257
Enter our friend, the Cavendish banana

00:21:03.806 --> 00:21:07.084
♪ God save the precious queen ♪

00:21:07.084 --> 00:21:08.059
Like I said at the beginning,

00:21:08.059 --> 00:21:10.783
this banana was invented by a
British guy who was tinkering

00:21:10.783 --> 00:21:14.012
with banana seeds in this
amazing looking house

00:21:14.012 --> 00:21:15.291
in Northern England.

00:21:15.291 --> 00:21:17.717
And after years of playing
around with the genetics,

00:21:17.717 --> 00:21:21.137
the plant finally flowered
and popped out this big,

00:21:21.137 --> 00:21:22.796
beautiful yellow banana.

00:21:22.796 --> 00:21:26.731
And so he named it after
the Duke William Cavendish.

00:21:26.731 --> 00:21:27.775
So now here's the sad part.

00:21:27.775 --> 00:21:29.323
The Gros Michel,

00:21:29.323 --> 00:21:31.627
the one that had caught on
that was like the big hit in

00:21:31.627 --> 00:21:34.558
America was a way better banana,

00:21:34.558 --> 00:21:35.864
is a way better banana.

00:21:35.864 --> 00:21:36.697
It is sweeter.

00:21:36.697 --> 00:21:39.702
It's more durable and it's
just overall a better banana

00:21:39.702 --> 00:21:41.193
compared to the Cavendish.

00:21:41.193 --> 00:21:42.026
In fact,

00:21:42.026 --> 00:21:44.878
the artificial banana flavor
that we have in our candies,

00:21:44.878 --> 00:21:48.852
like Laffy Taffy comes from
the original Gros Michel,

00:21:48.852 --> 00:21:50.015
not from the Cavendish,

00:21:50.015 --> 00:21:53.408
but the Gros Michel was getting
destroyed by this disease.

00:21:53.408 --> 00:21:56.995
And the Cavendish was luckily
resistant to the disease.

00:21:56.995 --> 00:22:00.510
So the dictator banana companies
made the switch and hoped

00:22:00.510 --> 00:22:03.104
that the people wouldn't care or notice

00:22:03.104 --> 00:22:04.421
as long as they looked the same.

00:22:04.421 --> 00:22:06.507
And guess what? It totally worked.

00:22:06.507 --> 00:22:09.590
They quietly switched out the
banana to the new Cavendish

00:22:09.590 --> 00:22:11.619
and people just kept buying bananas.

00:22:11.619 --> 00:22:12.452
I mean,

00:22:12.452 --> 00:22:14.191
I can't believe that there
is a better banana out there

00:22:14.191 --> 00:22:15.617
that I have not tasted.

00:22:15.617 --> 00:22:18.677
I wonder if I can get my hands on one.

00:22:18.677 --> 00:22:21.306
(upbeat music)

00:22:21.306 --> 00:22:24.117
The Gros Michel banana still exists.

00:22:24.117 --> 00:22:27.381
It's just a select few people
on this planet still grow it.

00:22:27.381 --> 00:22:29.481
And I found a farm that sent me a few.

00:22:29.481 --> 00:22:32.215
I'm going to try this thing.

00:22:32.215 --> 00:22:34.382
Gros Michel. Let's do this

00:22:39.644 --> 00:22:41.477
Beautiful banana. Wow.

00:22:44.036 --> 00:22:46.412
I'm obviously primed to like this more

00:22:46.412 --> 00:22:48.091
so I don't know if I'm
really liking it more,

00:22:48.091 --> 00:22:51.103
but it's definitely a better
banana right now in my mind.

00:22:51.103 --> 00:22:52.941
Slightly different color.

00:22:52.941 --> 00:22:55.386
It's got this like burgundy vibe going on.

00:22:55.386 --> 00:22:57.537
It's sweeter. I like the texture more.

00:22:57.537 --> 00:22:58.951
I think this is a better banana.

00:22:58.951 --> 00:23:02.963
Too bad this thing got completely
wiped out by a disease.

00:23:02.963 --> 00:23:05.765
So let's get up to speed
before we finish the story off,

00:23:05.765 --> 00:23:08.625
because the last part of
it is quite interesting

00:23:08.625 --> 00:23:11.629
and applies to our modern day.

00:23:11.629 --> 00:23:12.462
To summarize,

00:23:12.462 --> 00:23:15.697
came to the US, predatory
companies invaded whole countries

00:23:15.697 --> 00:23:18.455
to help the US exploit them for bananas

00:23:18.455 --> 00:23:20.904
while using amazing
marketing to keep demand up.

00:23:20.904 --> 00:23:23.234
And because of this Panama Disease,

00:23:23.234 --> 00:23:25.756
they had to switch to the
inferior Cavendish banana.

00:23:25.756 --> 00:23:26.589
So where do we go from here?

00:23:26.589 --> 00:23:29.451
United fruit eventually
rebranded to Chiquita.

00:23:29.451 --> 00:23:31.792
They're still doing
some pretty shady stuff

00:23:31.792 --> 00:23:34.273
like paying millions
of dollars to terrorist

00:23:34.273 --> 00:23:36.487
death squads and getting sued by workers

00:23:36.487 --> 00:23:39.458
who've become sterile
after handling pesticides.

00:23:39.458 --> 00:23:41.816
Banana companies will
probably always do this.

00:23:41.816 --> 00:23:43.516
Because the other thing that remains

00:23:43.516 --> 00:23:45.448
is that bananas are still soft and

00:23:45.448 --> 00:23:47.848
spoil quickly and grow in far away places

00:23:47.848 --> 00:23:49.914
from where you eat them.

00:23:49.914 --> 00:23:52.408
So the entire supply chain
needs to be controlled.

00:23:52.408 --> 00:23:54.919
And the other thing that
hasn't changed is that the new

00:23:54.919 --> 00:23:57.604
banana is genetically optimized

00:23:57.604 --> 00:23:59.860
so that every banana is exactly the same.

00:23:59.860 --> 00:24:01.746
So it fits perfectly into these boxes,

00:24:01.746 --> 00:24:03.233
into these crates, onto these ships,

00:24:03.233 --> 00:24:04.720
into these supermarkets.

00:24:04.720 --> 00:24:08.258
But guess what? There's another disease.

00:24:08.258 --> 00:24:09.146
The Panama disease,

00:24:09.146 --> 00:24:11.086
the one that wiped out the Gros Michel,

00:24:11.086 --> 00:24:13.245
it's back and it's mutated,

00:24:13.245 --> 00:24:15.152
and it's coming for the Cavendish.

00:24:15.152 --> 00:24:17.676
And what's tricky about these
diseases is there's no way to

00:24:17.676 --> 00:24:21.131
detect them before they
arrive before it's too late.

00:24:21.131 --> 00:24:23.482
It just quietly kills the banana trees

00:24:23.482 --> 00:24:26.481
and makes it impossible
to ever grow there again,

00:24:26.481 --> 00:24:28.198
you have to burn the entire plantation.

00:24:28.198 --> 00:24:30.643
This is already happening
in the Philippines,

00:24:30.643 --> 00:24:32.851
which is the second largest
exporter of bananas,

00:24:32.851 --> 00:24:36.595
the disease has devastated the
land and the banana economy.

00:24:36.595 --> 00:24:38.563
It's making its way through Asia.

00:24:38.563 --> 00:24:40.662
But Asia is super far away and it's like,

00:24:40.662 --> 00:24:42.610
there's a whole ocean in between.

00:24:42.610 --> 00:24:45.393
All our bananas come from
Latin America. Wrong.

00:24:45.393 --> 00:24:46.226
In 2019,

00:24:46.226 --> 00:24:48.309
it was detected in Colombia.

00:24:48.309 --> 00:24:50.458
And in April of this year,
just a few months ago,

00:24:50.458 --> 00:24:54.083
the disease was found in
Peru, right next to Ecuador,

00:24:54.083 --> 00:24:58.388
which is the largest exports
of bananas that come to the US.

00:24:58.388 --> 00:24:59.779
It's coming for your bananas.

00:24:59.779 --> 00:25:01.880
So everyone's on high alert and bananas

00:25:01.880 --> 00:25:03.829
as we know them might be doomed.

00:25:03.829 --> 00:25:07.367
So now we're seeing history
kind of repeat itself.

00:25:07.367 --> 00:25:09.179
We're looking at the potential collapse

00:25:09.179 --> 00:25:13.752
of a $25 billion industry
and entire economies,

00:25:13.752 --> 00:25:16.524
which is like hundreds
of thousands of jobs.

00:25:16.524 --> 00:25:17.987
(dramatic music)

00:25:17.987 --> 00:25:19.164
To me, the big problem here,

00:25:19.164 --> 00:25:22.393
the thing that's repeating
itself is the same problem

00:25:22.393 --> 00:25:25.817
of United Fruit Company going
in and trying to control

00:25:25.817 --> 00:25:29.418
every angle of their
production to maximize profits.

00:25:29.418 --> 00:25:33.751
When we have companies
that feel emboldened

00:25:33.751 --> 00:25:36.360
to do whatever it takes
to make the most profit,

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:38.125
you're going to get situations like this,

00:25:38.125 --> 00:25:41.351
whether it is banana massacres in Columbia

00:25:41.351 --> 00:25:43.319
or a coup in Honduras

00:25:43.319 --> 00:25:46.356
or a monoculture that
gets wiped out by disease

00:25:46.356 --> 00:25:47.638
time and time again,

00:25:47.638 --> 00:25:50.473
we try to engineer our
way out of these problems

00:25:50.473 --> 00:25:53.207
and we sometimes do, but
at the end of the day,

00:25:53.207 --> 00:25:55.186
nature will catch up with us.

00:25:55.186 --> 00:25:59.532
And as long as we ignore the
true cost of these economies,

00:25:59.532 --> 00:26:03.615
the cost to people, to
democracies, to ecologies,

00:26:04.509 --> 00:26:09.208
we're never actually going
to get rid of these problems.

00:26:09.208 --> 00:26:10.970
Thanks for watching.

00:26:10.970 --> 00:26:13.720
I'm going to eat a Cavendish now.

00:26:22.439 --> 00:26:23.272
See you.

